AU  - Wang, Meiling
AU  - Zhang, Nicholas
AU  - Chen, Renhai
AU  - Li, Ying
T2  - Proceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on the Foundations of Software Engineering
AB  - Modern software systems operate at unprecedented scale and complexity, making effective failure management both critical and increasingly challenging. Metrics, traces, and logs provide complementary views of runtime behavior, yet existing approaches typically rely on task-specific pipelines that tightly couple modality-specific preprocessing, representation learning, and downstream models, limiting generalization across tasks and systems. To address this gap, we propose RuntimeSlicer, a unified runtime state representation model for generalizable failure management. RuntimeSlicer pre-trains a task-agnostic model that directly encodes metrics, traces, and logs into a single aligned system-state embedding capturing the overall runtime condition. To train RuntimeSlicer, we introduce Unified Runtime Contrastive Learning, which integrates heterogeneous training data and optimizes complementary objectives for cross-modality alignment and temporal consistency. Building on the learned embeddings, we further propose State-Aware Task-Oriented Tuning, which partitions runtime states in an unsupervised manner and enables state-conditioned adaptation for downstream tasks. Preliminary experiments on the AIOps 2022 dataset demonstrate the feasibility and effectiveness of RuntimeSlicer for system state modeling and failure management.
