TI  - Fault Prediction in Large-Scale Cluster Environments Based on Improved TCN and DenseNet
AU  - Liu, Chongyang
AU  - Yuan, Shuai
AU  - Zheng, Yu
AU  - Su, Jia
T3  - CAICE '25
AB  - Cluster node fault prediction is an important issue in the field of AIOps, and various methods have been proposed to solve it. However, these proposed approaches face limitations in prediction performance because they fail to fully consider the unique characteristics of cluster metric data.  To tackle issues of noisy data and repetitive information in cluster time series, we propose a cluster fault prediction model called Dense-TiCN which combines DenseNet with an improved Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN). The model uses feature reuse in DenseBlocks to improve robustness against noisy data, use TCN  with channel attention mechanisms to make it effective to capture long-term dependencies in time series data and amplify key features. These advancements considerably improve the precision and reliability of forecasts. Experimental results indicate that the Dense-TiCN model outperforms conventional fault prediction methods in terms of both precision and efficiency. It achieves a prediction precision of 95.6% and an F1 score exceeding 0.92. This work demonstrates the potential of combining DenseNet and TCN with channel attention to handle complex, noisy time series data in cluster environments, providing an efficient and accurate solution for large-scale cluster fault prediction.
